// Production-path effects-rack tests for the real synth patch main.
// These tests intentionally exercise WavetableSynth.cmajorpatch rather than a probe
// graph: what they pin is the INTEGRATION - that the rack, the fixed output stage and
// the rewired analyzers reach real DSP through the whole instrument.
//
// THE GOLDENS ARE INDEPENDENT, WHICH IS THE ONLY REASON THESE TESTS MEAN ANYTHING.
// expectedOutput-audioOut.wav in fixtures/rack_default_inert and in
// fixtures/rack_shipping_chain_identity_order was rendered by the PRE-RACK patch -
// the fixed `trim -> distortion -> chorus -> audioOut` tail - before a line of this
// integration existed, and was never regenerated afterwards. A golden regenerated from
// the code under test proves only self-consistency; these two prove that the code under
// test still sounds like the instrument that shipped. Every other fixture below reuses
// one of those two files verbatim, so the entire suite is anchored to pre-rack audio.
//
// WHY THE NOTE STARTS AT FRAME 1024. A structure upload costs one ADR-003 transition:
// the rack fades to exact silence, swaps, and fades back, which at 44.1 kHz is 2 x 132
// frames. Uploading at frame 0 therefore leaves the rack at exact unity from frame 264
// onwards, long before the note sounds. The fixtures are built that way so that a
// difference in the rendered audio can only be the REORDERING, never the fade - the
// transition itself is pinned frame-by-frame in RackShape.cmajtest, where it belongs.
//
// WHAT EACH CASE PINS:
//   1. rack_default_inert                  - the rack in the path changes nothing.
//   2. rack_all_disabled_dry               - hard audible bypass, at patch level.
//   3. rack_rejected_order                 - a malformed order is rejected, not coerced.
//   4. rack_shipping_chain_identity_order  - moving Distortion and Chorus into the rack
//                                            preserved the shipping sound.
//   5. rack_order_distortion_then_chorus   - an explicit reorder that preserves the two
//                                            enabled modules' relative order preserves
//                                            the sound, against that same pre-rack golden.
//   6. rack_order_chorus_then_distortion   - swapping just those two changes it.

const patchPath = "../../WavetableSynth.cmajorpatch";
const defaultFrequency = 44100;
const defaultBlockSize = 64;
const samplesToRender = 8192;

// -300 dB is bit-exactness in float32, not a tolerance. The rack's claim is that its
// dry paths are EXACT, so the tests state it that way; anything looser would let a
// silently-wrong forward or a stray coefficient hide under the floor.
const bitExactDiffDb = -300.0;

// Compares every output the patch has, including the two distortion analyzers, which
// now read preview streams forwarded through the rack rather than reaching into
// wt::DistortionBus directly. That rewiring is invisible in the audio and would
// otherwise go unasserted.
function fullPatchRun(subDir)
{
    return {
        patch: patchPath,
        subDir,
        frequency: defaultFrequency,
        blockSize: defaultBlockSize,
        samplesToRender,
        sessionID: 1,
        skipMissingInputs: true,
        maxDiffDb: bitExactDiffDb
    };
}

// Audio plus effectiveRackState - what the rack committed and what it rejected - for
// the four fixtures whose other outputs have no independent pre-rack baseline to be
// compared against and would therefore only be asserting against themselves.
function audioPatchRun(subDir)
{
    let options = fullPatchRun (subDir);
    options.skipMissingOutputs = true;
    return options;
}

// 1. INERTNESS. Default rack: identity order, OTT off, every Mix/Wet at its dry init.
//    Eight resident modules are advancing every frame and wt::RackOutputStage is
//    shaping every sample, and the instrument is bit-identical to the one that had
//    neither. Compared across ALL outputs, not just audio.

## runScript (fullPatchRun("fixtures/rack_default_inert"))

// 2. HARD AUDIBLE BYPASS (ADR-005). Same render, but every module's wet control is
//    driven hard - lowpass at 200 Hz with resonance 8, 30 dB of distortion at full wet,
//    chorus/flanger/phaser/delay/reverb all at mix 1.0 with feedback 0.9 - and every
//    module is disabled by one rackEnable upload. Bit-identical to the dry golden.
//    A disabled module passes the signal through EXACTLY; it does not merely go quiet.

## runScript (audioPatchRun("fixtures/rack_all_disabled_dry"))

// 3. STRUCTURE VALIDATION (rack invariant C11). The uploaded order (1,1,2,3,4,5,6,7)
//    names module 1 twice and module 0 never. A module advanced zero times per frame
//    silently re-emits its previous frame - a corrupted time base with no error attached
//    - so the upload is rejected outright. effectiveRackState reports rejectedOrderCount
//    1 with committedStructureGeneration still 0 and committedOrderCode still the
//    identity permutation, and the audio is bit-identical to the dry golden because the
//    running order was left completely intact. Rejected, never coerced.

## runScript (audioPatchRun("fixtures/rack_rejected_order"))

// 4. THE SHIPPING CHAIN STILL SOUNDS LIKE ITSELF. Distortion at full wet and Chorus at
//    mix 0.6, no rack uploads at all, against a golden rendered by the pre-rack patch
//    with the same parameter values through the fixed distortion -> chorus tail. This is
//    the assertion that moving two shipping effects into a runtime-ordered rack was a
//    refactor and not a rewrite. Compared across ALL outputs.

## runScript (fullPatchRun("fixtures/rack_shipping_chain_identity_order"))

// 5. A REORDER REACHES REAL DSP, AND PRESERVES WHAT IT SHOULD. Same parameters, with
//    rackOrder (1,3,0,2,4,5,6,7) uploaded at frame 0: Distortion moves to position 0 and
//    Chorus to position 1, with the six dry modules behind them. Their RELATIVE order is
//    unchanged, and the six modules that moved are bit-exact passthroughs, so the audio
//    must still equal the pre-rack shipping-chain golden exactly. This is the case that
//    catches a rack which corrupts the signal simply by being reordered.

## runScript (audioPatchRun("fixtures/rack_order_distortion_then_chorus"))

// 6. ...AND SWAPPING THE TWO CHANGES THE SOUND. Byte-for-byte the inputs of case 5 with
//    one field different - rackOrder (3,1,0,2,4,5,6,7), Chorus now ahead of Distortion.
//    This is the suite's ONLY golden rendered by the code under test, and the pairing is
//    what stops that from being circular: it differs from case 5's pre-rack golden by
//    maxDiff 0.0291 against a 0.4123 peak, i.e. -23.0 dB - 277 dB above the floor these
//    tests assert at - so a rack that ignored rackOrder would render cases 5 and 6
//    identically and could then match at most one of the two goldens.
//    expectedOutput-effectiveRackState.json pins the other half: the DSP committed the
//    exact permutation that was uploaded - 16434187, the eight module ids packed three
//    bits per position, least-significant-position first - rather than coercing it, and
//    committed it on frame 132, which is ADR-003's 3 ms transition at 44.1 kHz.

## runScript (audioPatchRun("fixtures/rack_order_chorus_then_distortion"))
